      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 31,
      "player": "Llama4 Spike",
      "decision_index": 24,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Emperor of Bones exiles a card from a graveyard at the beginning of combat, and when it gets +1/+1 counters (via Adapt 2 for 1B), it puts an exiled creature onto the battlefield with haste and a finality counter. Exiling Psychic Frog (a 1/2 for UB) is far weaker than exiling Archon of Cruelty (a 6/6 flying creature that triggers 'sacrifice a creature, discard, lose 3 life, draw a card, gain 3 life' on ETB). If Emperor of Bones adapts later, reanimating Archon of Cruelty would be massively more impactful than a Psychic Frog. Even though Archon costs 6BB normally, Emperor of Bones puts the exiled creature directly onto the battlefield regardless of mana cost.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Psychic Frog with Emperor of Bones' combat trigger.",
      "better_line": "Exile Archon of Cruelty with Emperor of Bones. When Emperor adapts (1B), you'd get a 6/6 flying Archon that triggers its devastating ETB ability, which would likely swing the game immediately - forcing opponent to sacrifice a creature, discard, lose 3 life while you draw and gain 3.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 40,
      "player": "MiniMx Villain",
      "decision_index": 33,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Undercity Sewers instead of Swamp means the land enters tapped, so no mana is available this turn. With two Ruin Crabs on the battlefield, playing the Swamp (which enters untapped) would trigger both Ruin Crabs to mill 6 cards total from the opponent, AND leave mana open. Undercity Sewers also triggers the Ruin Crabs and provides a surveil, but it enters tapped so no mana is available. The surveil is marginal value since the hand is just a Swamp after this. Playing Swamp now and Undercity Sewers next turn would give the same total mill triggers over two turns but leave mana available this turn (relevant for potential plays off the top or holding up interaction). However, the hand has no castable spells regardless, so the main downside is just having untapped mana available. The surveil from Undercity Sewers does put a card in graveyard which could matter for Cephalid Coliseum threshold (currently 5 cards in graveyard, need 7). Playing Undercity Sewers and surveilling a card to graveyard would put graveyard at 6, still not threshold. Playing Swamp keeps the option of playing Undercity Sewers next turn for surveil when closer to threshold. This is a marginal sequencing decision but playing Swamp untapped is slightly better to have mana available.",
      "action_taken": "Played Undercity Sewers (enters tapped) instead of Swamp",
